why 5 1/8 PROGRESSION Over the bass's held E the soprano and alto move in eighths, so these chord changes are passing ones on the way to I⁶.
why 5 1/2 DOUBLING Soprano (A–B) and tenor (C♯–B) meet on B by step from opposite sides.
why 6 0 PROGRESSION Over the bass's held D the alto and tenor step down G–F♯–E and B–A–G: a neighbor ⁶₄ that settles onto the D chord. Over a held bass it is a decoration, not a step backward.
why 7 1/4 SIMILAR_MOTION All four voices step down together into a diminished chord heading for E minor, led by the bass's walk G–F♯–E–D♯–E.
why 7 1/2 LEADING_TONE Here F♯ is a suspension over E minor, not a leading tone; it falls to E, as a suspension should.
why 9 3/4 DOUBLING This is the cadential ⁶₄ of a cadence in A major (an A chord over E). Soprano and tenor share C♯, its third: the soprano falls C♯–B and the tenor C♯–A, so neither has to rise.
